# Bilingual judgment scope

Compare every required unit in the frozen approved English source and directly
authored Russian candidate. Judge complete documents, reading-order units and
isolated values in their actual roles. Audit inventory coverage and grouping
against supplied complete documents. Respect contextual headings and genuinely
inseparable accessible groups; do not demand unrelated repetition.

Check facts, actors, objects, operations, causal links, order, conditions, scope,
limitations, units, terminology, history, optional tasks, answers, handoffs and
accessibility meaning. Preserve formulas, Rust, API names, code regions, values,
literal output, links and immutable identifiers. Compare meaning, not sentence
shape. Report an English ambiguity as a source blocker rather than silently
resolving it through translation.

This is full direct localization of Chapter 40's fixed scalar-reference input
identity and scope-bearing report. It does not change the decoder or upgrade
the reference to a useful laptop model. Preserve the distinctions between input
identity and checked behavior, per-schedule and aggregate training work,
overlapping target slots and positional transition occurrences, same-seed
replay and seed variation, narrow state reload and complete job continuation,
and generation-path agreement and language quality. Future Chapter 41 remains
future work. Internal delivery instructions are not learner prose.

Use only the routed immutable inputs. Do not edit or obtain another role's
private judgment. A clean pass is valid; do not request preference-only changes.
A blocker must cite affected surface IDs and supplied evidence, explain the
meaning change and learner consequence, and state a correction criterion.
Return only the schema-required record with every required surface covered
exactly once. Preserve the compact JSON, UTF-8 key/ID ordering and exact final
LF required by the routed prompt and schema. The appended frozen role map
defines neutral obligations, not expected judgments.

